# GC183 Common urological malignancies and their presentations

- **Presenting problems:**
  - Haematuria
  - Raised PSA

## Haematuria

- **Definition** - Bloody urine in lay terms, but actually defined as presence of abnormal amounts of RBC in urine microscopy (differentiate from haemoglobinuria, myoglobinuria)
- **Epidemiology** - accounts for 20% adult urology consultations
- **Classification:**
  - Gross/ visible (VH) - painless gross haematuria is cancer until proven otherwise, always demands complete workup
  - Microscopic/ non-visible (NVH) - \>= 3 RBC per high-power field on microscopic examination (2.4-31.1\$ among healthy volunter, but risk of cancer 1-3%, mostly bladder cancer)
- **Dipstick haematuria** - presence of Hb in urine:
  - Modality of testing - relies on peroxidase activity of Hb on a chromogen on dipstick:
    - False +ve - menstrual blood, haemoglobinuria, myoglobinuria, dehydration
    - False -ve - urinw with high SG, high levels of ascorbic acid, nitrites, pH \< 5, proteinuria
    - 20-74% dipstick +ve patients have normal urine microscopy
- **DDx of Haematuria:**
  - Contamination - menstrual blood contamination
  - Dehydration - concentrated urine
  - Obstructive jaundice - bilirubinaemia
  - Haemoglobinuria - intravascular haemolysis (PNH)
  - Myoglobinuria - rhabdomyolysis
  - Pigments in urine - Betanin (Beeturia), Azo (Phenazopyridine, pyridium), Rifampicin
  - **True haematuria:**
    - Neoplastic - RCC, urothelial carcinoma, prostate cancer, angiomyolipoma
    - Infective - UTI, GUTB, schistosomiasis
    - Stones - Renal, ureteric, bladder stones
    - Glomerular - ddx of glomerular bleeding
    - Others - ADPKD, Bleeding BPH, irradiation cystitis, bleeding tendancies
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - HPI - onset, duration, gross vs microscopic, painful vs painful, timing (not reliable), exacerbating factors (exercise)
  - Associated Sx:
    - Fever and dysuria
    - Loin pain
    - Strangury
    - ?LUTS?
  - SHx - smoking Hx, occupational exposure to carcinogens
  - FHx = ADPKD, urolithiasis
  - Drug Hx - Anticoagulants
- P/E - typically unremarkable
- Ix:
  - Routine baseline Ix - CBC, RFT
  - Urine - dipstick?, urine microscopy, Urine C/ST
  - +/- Early morning urine - for TB
  - Mandatory Ix for gross haematuria:
    - Cytoscopic examination of urinary bladder - flexible mainstay now
    - Upper tract imaging - US, IV urogram, CT urogram (mainstay), MR urogram, Retrograde pyelography
- Limitations of urine cytology - not sensitive:
  - Detect abnormal epithelial cells
  - Only +ve in high grade cancer or CIS, -ve in low grade bladder cancers:
    - High specificity (96%)
  - Results - last 2 results require further workup
    - Unsatisfactory for examination
    - -ve for maligant cells
    - Atypical cells
    - Suspicious for malignancy
    - Carcinoma cells present
- IVU - time honored Ix (scount, nephrgram, 5 min, 10 min, PM, ?72h)
  - Information regarding function - assumes normal function of kidneys for excretion of contrast
  - Direct evidence of obstruction - upholding of contrast by obstruction
  - Disadvantages - contrast, small tumours in parenchyma missed
- Contrast CT/ CTU (gold standard):
  - Difference between Contrast CT and CTU - added delayed (urogram phases) to outline ureters –\> visualise filling defects
- MR urography:
  - Indications - avoid irradiation (e.g. pregnancy, contrast allergy, nephrotoxicity)
  - Not first line because inferior imaging for organs moving on respiration (i.e. kidneys), and poor images on stones (appears as signal voids)
- Retrograde pyelogram:
  - Injection of contrast retrograde via cystoscopy
  - Indications - renal failure (failed excretion of contrast by kidneys to outline urinary tract)
  - +/- Intervention - stenting to relieve obstruction
- Indications for full workup:
  - All gross haematuria - esp. if painless
  - FOr microscopic haematuria - AUA 2020 guidelines for risk stratification (based on age, smoking, microscopy, other risk factors):
    - Low risk - repeat urinalysis in 6mo, or cystoscopy + USG kidneys
    - Intermediate risk - cystoscopy and USG kidneys
    - High risk - cystoscopy + CT urogram

## Renal cell carcinoma

- Epidemiology:
  - Incidence - rising
- Pathology - most commonly clear cell type (paraneoplastic syndrome, VTE, hereditary cancer syndromes e.g. VHL)
- Clinical presentation:
  - Internest tumour - presents to medical due to myriad of (paraneoplastic) Sx
  - Radiologists tumour - incidental finding
  - Clinical triad (very very rare nowadays) - loin pain, haematuria, palpable mass
- Mx of localised RCC:
  - Surgical extirpation - partial nephrrectomy/ radical nephrectomy
  - Ablation - RFA, cryotherapy
- Mx of metastatic RCC:
  - Immune checkpoint - Pembrolizumab, nivolumab, ipilimumab
  - TKI - pazopanib, sunitinib, sorafenib, axitinib

## Bladder cancer

- Risk factors - smoking, carcinogen exposure
- Classification by staging:
  - Non-muscle invasive - CIS, Ta, T1
  - Muscle invasive - T2 or beyond
- Staging by TUR (transurethrial resection) - T staging:
  - If TURBT staging - muscle invasive –\> proceed to Mx (radical cystectomy)
  - If TURBT staging non muscle invasive –\> second look TURBT to avoid under-staging
  - Immediate instillation of MMC after each TURBT to prevent recurrence
- Prognosis:
  - High recurrence rates - 20-90% at 1y:
    - Requires regular surveillance cystoscopy - q3mo at 1y, a6mo at 2y, q1y afterwards
  - Risk fators of high recurrence and high progression:
    - T1 high grade disease
    - CIS disease
  - May consider early radical systectomy or BCG induction and maintenance
- Mx of muscle invasive Ca bladder:
  - Standard of care - radical cystectomy + urinary diversion (radiotherapy is inferior)
  - Options for urinary diversion:
    - Continent - neobladder, catheterisable pouch
    - Incontinent - ileal pouch
  - 2y mortality 85% if without Tx

## Upper tract urothelial carcinoma (UTUC)

- Epidemiology:
  - Disease of elderly
- Risk factors:
  - Smoking
  - Exposure - Aristolochia fangchi (chinese herb nephropathy), Aristolochic acid (Balkan endemic nephropathy), Arsenic poisoning (blackfoot disease)
  - HNPCC
- Ix:
  - Retrograde pyelogram - reveals goblet sign (cupping of contrast due to obstruction)
- Mx:
  - Standard of care - nephrouretectomy
  - Note high rates of bladder recurrence –\> surveillance
  - Alternative - partial uretectomy followed by reconstruction –\> surveillance ureteroscopy

## Elevated PSA

- **Physiology of PSA:**
  - <u>Synthesis and secretion of PSA</u> - synthesis exclusive by **prostate epithelial cells** in an **androgen-dependent manner**, released into seminal fluids mainly (mg/dL), with minor amounts released into circulation (ng/dL)
  - <u>Physiological function of PSA</u> - serine protease encoded by hKLK3 gene that liquefies semenogelin and fibronectin after ejaculation to maintain liquid state
  - PSA expression considered exclusive in prostate epithelial cells –\> prostate-specific (but not antigen specific)
- **PSA as a biomarker** - elevation arbitrarily defined as <u>\> 4 ng/dL</u>:
  - <u>Principles of elevation of PSA</u> - caused by disruption of histological architecture of prostate by disease (cancer due to BM disruption) or manipulation (Bx)
  - <u>Factors of PSA secretion independent of disease</u>:
    - Age - 0.04 ng/ml/y
    - Race - black race
    - Prostate volume
  - <u>Role of PSA as biomarker</u> - at beast an organ-specific marker, but not cancer-specific
- **Emerging markers related to PSA:**
  - Age-specific PSA - offers age-specific cut-off range
  - PSA density - Serum levels divided by prostate volume (TRUS) to differentiate between prostate cancer and BPH
  - PSA veolicity - rate of change of PSA over time (alternative to PSA doubling time)
  - Free/ total PSA ratio \[f/t PSA\] - postulated that prostate cancer has a lower percentage of free PSA compared w/ benign conditions
  - Prostate health index \[phi\] - risk score taking in acount of \[-2\]proPSA (isoform of proPSA synthesised by cancer cells) and free PSA
- **DDx of elevated PSA:**
  - Prostate cancer - typically more marked increase due to interuption of basemement membrane facilitating release into circulation
  - BPH
  - Prostatitis
  - AROU
  - Prostate trauma - Bx, massage, surgery
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - <u>What is the reason for PSA testing in the first place?</u> - health check, or in the setting of clinical suspicion of prostate cancer
  - <u>Are there any confounders for PSA testing?</u>
    - **Urinary tract infections and prostatitis** - dysuria
    - Acute rentention of urine - inability to pass urine
    - Benigh prostatic hyperplasia - Lower urinary tract symptoms
  - <u>Are there alarming features of prostate cancer?</u>
    - **Gross haematuria** - clinical suspicion of urinary tract malignancy until proven otherwise
    - **Constitutional Sx** - weight loss
    - **Features of metastatic disease** - back pain, bone pain, lower limb weakness
- **P/E:**
  - <u>General examination</u> - uraemic features, cachexia
  - <u>Targeted neurological examination</u> - LL weakness
  - <u>Abdomen examination</u> - distended bladder, ballotable kidneys
  - <u>DRE</u> - induration, nodules, median sulcus, size and consistency of prostate
- **Diagnostic evaluation:**
  - **Elevated PSA in the absence of identifiable confounding factors** - proceed with <u>prostate biopsy</u> after shared decision making w/ patients
  - **Spurious elevated PSA** - repeat PSA after 4 weeks
- **Principles of prostate Bx** - currently transrectal ultrasound-guided prostate biopsy (TRUS-Bx) is gold standard, but there are recent advancements with lowered risks:
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - 1\. Elevated PSA w/o confounders
    - 2\. Suspicious DRE findings
  - <u>Procedures</u>:
    - **Transrectal ultrasound-guided biopsy** (TRUS-Bx) - systematic template Bx with variable number of cores (8-12) under transrectal US guidance (under ABx prophylaxis and LA)
    - **MRI/USG fusion-targeted biopsy** (potentially higher cancer detection rate) - multiparametric MRI and grading of suspicious lesions on PIRADs system with fusion of images into TRUS images for targeted biopsy
    - **Transperitineal biopsy** - systematic Bx via transperitoneal route w/ close to zero sepsis rate
  - <u>Complications</u>:
    - **Mild complications** - PR bleeding, haematuria, dysuria, transient increased LUTS
    - **Severe complications** - Post-biopsy infection (PBI), acute retention of urine, vasovagal syncope
  - <u>Post-biopsy infection</u> - occurs in 4.3% of TRUS-Bx (1.8% in MRI/USG fusion-targeted Bx):
    - **Pathophysiology** - Translocation of rectal bacteria into systemic circulation, which is not fully cleared by ABx prophylaxis, resulting in sepsis
    - **Onset** - within 1 week (most within 24h)

## Prostate cancer

- Clinical staging:
  - MSKCC prostate cancer Nomograms - combine PSA, Gleason scores, and clinical staging (DRE), and Bx results
  - Radiologic imaging:
    - PSMA-PET scan (PSMA as staging) - for intermidiate to high risk disease to exclude metastatic disease
    - MRI - size, configuration, extraprostatic extension
  - Pelvic lymphadenectomy
- Stratification by staging:
  - Organ confined
  - Locally advanced
  - Metastatic disease
  - Castration resistant
- Mx:
  - Organ confined disease (curative intent) - radical prostatectomy, radiotherapy
  - Locally advanced - radical prostatectomy + post-op RT/ADT +/- neoadjuvant
  - Metastic - androgen deprivation therapy + cytotoxic chemotherapy/ AR-targeted therapy

# GC210 Urinary Tract Infection

- UTI - defined as inflammatory response of the urothelium to invasion by bacteria or other pathogens, usually a/w bacteriuria and pyuria
- Epidemiology - one of most common bacterial infection:
  - Prevalence - 1/3 F have 1 UTI by 24 (\> 50% lifetime risk in F)
- Overlap in symptomatology with patients with chronic pelvic pain syndrome, over-active bladder, IC, BPH/LUTS
- Terminology:
  - Bacteriuria - presence of bacteria in urine (does not equate infection):
    - DDx - colonisation or contamination
    - Symptomatic vs asymptomatic
  - Pyuria - presence of WBC in urine, indicative of infection/ inflammatory response (does not equate infection)
    - DDx - Urolithiasis, Malignancy, TB
- Pathophysiology - interaction between host and uropathogens:
  - Host factors - inherent susceptibility, defense mechanisms:
    - Defense mechanisms - normal flora (lactobacilli produces low vaginal pH), cervical IgA, antegrade flow of urine (physical and chemical characteristics, pH, high osmolality, Tamm-Horsfall protein), normal emptying of bladder allows exfoliation of urothelial
    - Susceptibility:
      - Vaginal cell susceptibility - to bacterial adherance
      - Lewis blood group 0 determines ability of cellular fucosylaion, affects adherance (non-secretory status a/w pre-menopausal acute pyelonephritis)
  - Uropathogen factors - virulence, inoculum size:
    - Adherence - fimbrae (e.g. mannose-resistance pili more prone to acute pyelonephritis)
    - Toxins - haemolysin A
    - Urease - urea into ammonia (stones)
    - Bacterial adherence –\> colonoization/ ascending infection –\> bacterial invasion and clinical UTI
  - Routes of infection:
    - Ascending infection - rectum –\> urethra –\> bladder, facilitated by indwelling catheter, ureteric obstruction, and vesicoureteric reflux
    - Haematogenous - spread from extra-renal source of bacteremia (GUTB)
    - Direct extension - from neighboring source
- Microbiology - most are facultative anaerobes from bowel flora:
  - Etiology of community acquired UTI - E. coli (85%), Proteus spp, Klebsiella spp, Enterococcus, Staphylococcus saprophyticus
  - Etiology of nosocomial UTI - E. coli (50%), pseudomonas, citrobacter, Providencia, Serratia etc.
- Clinical types of UTI:
  - Anatomical classification:
    - Cystitis
    - Pyelonephritis
    - Pyonephrosis
    - Renal abscess
    - Prostatis
    - Epididymo-orchitis
    - Urethritis/ STD
  - By population:
    - Uncomplicated UTI - in anatomically physiologically normal urinary tracts w/ normal host defense mechanism
    - Complicated UTI - secondary to urological pathology, e.g. neuropathic bladder, urinary tract obstruction, bladder diverticulum, stones (increased risk of recurrent infection and failing Tx)
    - Special groups - pregnancy and children
- Workup for suspected UTI:
  - Hx:
    - Associated gross haematuria
    - Previous antibiotic Tx
    - Presence of complicated features - e.g. stones, previous surgery to urinary tract, neurogenic bladder
    - Recurrent UTI - defined as \>=2 in 6mo or \>=3 in 1y
    - Social Hx and drug Hx –\> screen for ketamine use
  - Ix:
    - Urinalysis + Urine C/ST
      - Urinalysis:
        - Gross examination - appearance (cloudy)
        - Biochemical examination - pH, specific gravity, osmolality, leukocyte esterase, nitrates
        - Microscopic examination - pyuria, RBC (casts, or dysmorphic)
          - Pyuria - defined as \> 10 WBC/mm3
          - DDx of sterile bactiuria:
            - GUTB - save EMU
            - Bladder cancer
            - Urinary tract stones
            - Schistomsomiasis
            - Partially treated UTI
            - Other inflammatory bladder conditions - e.g. interstital cystitis, ketamine cystitis
      - Urine C/ST:
        - Specimen - Mid-stream urine (MSU):
          - Prone to contamination (1/3 contaminated in F patients)
        - Instructions:
          - F - spread labia and clean periurethral area with moist gauze back to front, catch MSU (10-15ml after initial 100-150ml void)
          - M - retract forskin (if uncircumcisede), wash glans with soap and water, catch MSU
        - Cutoff of +ve urine culture - traditionally \>= 10<sup>5</sup> CFU by definition, lower cutoff for symptomatic patients:
          - Uncomplicated cystititis - \>= 10<sup>3</sup> CFU
          - Uncomplicated pyelonephritis - \>= 10<sup>4</sup> CFU
    - +/- RFT, imaging (complicated UTI)
    - EAU guidelines on UTI imaging:
      - Uncomplicated cystitis in F - no role of imaging
      - Uncomplicated pyelonephritis in F - US (demonstrate stones/ hydronephrosis). CT kidneys (if S/S not improving in 72h), KUB for stones
      - UTI in men (most are complicated)
    - Mx:
      - Uncomplicated cystitis - ABx for 3d (except nitrofurantoin which should be given for 7d)
      - Uncomplicated pyelonephritis - Admission + IV ABx 10-14d

## Asymptomatic bacteriuria

- Definition - Defined \>= 10<sup>5</sup> in F and \>= 10<sup>3</sup> in M in the absence of Sx
- Does not equate clinical UTI
- Epidemiology:
  - Prevalence - 4-7% women
- Screening and Tx not indicated in:
  - Pre-penopausal non-pregnant women
  - Postmenopausal women
  - Men
  - Indwelling catheters
  - Nephrostomy tubes/ JJ stent
  - Spinal cord injury
- Screening and Tx of recommended in:
  - Pregnant F
  - Patient undergoing genitourinary procedure w/ risk of mucosal bleeding - e.g. TURP, lithotripsy
- Pregnancy and asymptomatic bacteriuria:
  - Incidence - similar to non-pregnant F
  - Pathophysiology - progression from asymptomatic bacteriuria and ascending infection (e.g. hydronephrosis and hydroureters)
  - Complications:
    - Upper tract infections - a/w prematurity and low birth rate
  - Mx - routine screening and Tx of asymptomatic bacteriuria
- Approach:
  - Differentiate w/ contaminated specimen (based on dipstick and microscopy results)

## Recurrent UTI

- Definition - documented resolution of previous one occuring at frequency of:
  - \>= 2 episodes in 6 mo
  - \>= 3 episodes in 1y
- Etio-pathophysiology:
  - Bacterial persistence/ relapse - recurrence of same organism from a foci within urinary tract (e.g. stones, bladder diverticulum)
  - Bacterial re-infection (\>95%) - recurrent UTI caused by different organisms or same organism but separated by documented period of no growth in urine
- Risk factors:
  - Pre-menopausal women:
    - Sexual intercourse
    - Use of spermicide and diaphragm
    - Pelvic anatomy - urethra to anus distance
    - Age of 1st UTI
    - +ve FHx of recurrent UTI
    - Prior anti-microbial use
  - Post-menopausal women:
    - Estrogen deficiency
    - Incontinence
    - Cystocele
    - Large post-void residual urine
    - Hx of UTI before menopause
    - Genetic factors

## Urinary Tract Infections

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Urinary tract infections</u> - clinical syndrome caused by bacterial infection at any level along the urinary tract
  - <u>Bacteriuria</u> - presence of bacteria in urine, which may reflect colonisation, contamination or actual infection
  - <u>Pyuria</u> - presence of white blood cell in urine, merely indicative of an inflammatory response within the urinary tract
- **Epidemiology** - extremely common infection:
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - \> 50% F will have \>= 1 episode of UTI in their lifetime
  - <u>Demographic</u>:
    - Age - 1/3 will have 1 episode by 24y
    - Sex - common in F, much less common in M
- **Natural defense of the urinary tract:**
  - <u>Physical factors</u>:
    - Normal emptying of bladder and anterograde flow of urine
    - Normal exfoliation of urothelial cells
    - Tight cell-cell junctions between urothelial cells
  - <u>Chemical factors</u> - high osmolality of protein, mucosal IgA, Tamm-Horsfall protein
  - <u>Microbiological factors</u> - presence of lactobacilli in vagina, introitus and periurethral area results in production of low pH (estrogen-dependent)
- **Etiological agents of Community acquired UTI:**
  - <u>Facultatively GNR</u> (bowel flora) - E. coli (85%), Proteus spp, Klebsiella spp
  - <u>GPC</u> - enterococcus, S. saprophyticus (10% of acute cystitis in sexually-active young women)
  - <u>Anaerobes</u> (capable of causing nosocomial infections)
- **Etiological agents of nosocomial UTI** - can be polymicrobial:
  - <u>GNR</u> - E. coli and other enterobacter spp, Pseudomonas, Citrobacter, Providencia, Serratia
  - <u>CNS</u> - e.g. S. epidermidis
- **Routes of infection:**
  - <u>Ascending route</u> (commonest) - ascends from rectum to urethra to bladder, and ocassionally to the kidneys (facilitated by BOO, vesicoureteral refluxes, or prolonged instrumentation)
  - <u>Haematogenous spread</u> (rare) - from pre-existing bacteraemia (e.g. S. aureus)
  - <u>Direct extension</u> - from neighboring suppurative infections
- **Classification of UTI by the host:**
  - <u>Uncomplicated UTI</u> - UTI occuring in an anatomically, physiologically urinary tract with normal host defense mechanisms
  - <u>Complicated UTI</u> - UTI occuring in an anatomically, or physiologically abnormal urinary tract that is a/w increased risk of acquiring infections or failing Tx
  - <u>Special groups</u> - UTI in pregnancy, UTI in children
- **Approach to uncomplicated v.s. complicated UTI:**
  - <u>Uncomplicated UTI</u> (a/w usual uropathogens) - Tx in out-patient setting with short-duration of ABx therapy
  - <u>Complicated UTI</u> - Tx in in-patient setting, likely with longer duration of ABx therapy, where cure w/o recurrence is only achieved if the complicating features is erradicated
- **Ix:**
  - **Urine** - dipstick, microscopy, biochemistries, and C/ST:
    - **Urine dipstick** - leukocyte esterase, nitrites:
      - <u>Leukocyte esterase</u> - +ve (LE produced by neutrophils)
      - <u>Nitrites</u> - +ve (most GNR convert nitrates into nitrites)
      - <u>Urinary pH</u> - elevated pH suggestive of infection by <u>urease-producing bacteria</u> that often complicates w/ struvite stone formation (e.g. proteus, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas)
    - **Urine microscopy** - for pyuria (\> 10 cells/ mm3) +/- microhaematuria (absence of squammous epithelial cells suggests absence of contamination)
    - **Urine culture and sensitivity testing** - from MSU (see technical details below)
  - **Urological imaging** - KUB +/- CT urogogram for evaluation of urinary tract in selected patients:
    - **Indications for urological evaluation:**
      - <u>UTI in women</u> - USG indicated in suspected pyelonephritis to r/o stones/ obstruction
      - <u>UTI in men</u> - full urological workup indicated for all men
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Determine clinical type of UTI</u> - cystitis vs acute pyelonephritis
    - <u>Determining cause of UTI</u> - identify complicated vs uncomplicated UTI
    - <u>Initiate ABx therapy</u> - empirical therapy dependent on the most likely organisms
  - **ABx therapy:**
    - <u>Uncomplicated cystitis</u> - short-course augmentin (3d) or prolonged course nitrofurantoin (7d)
    - <u>Acute pyelonephritis</u> - prolonged course augmentin/ tasozin/ ceftriazone (consider meropenem if no clinical improvement \[due to ESBL\])
- **Urine dipstick:**
  - **Leukocyte esterase** - enzyme expressed in neutrophils:
    - <u>Interpretation</u> - +ve results indicative of pyuria, i.e. active inflammation along the urinary tract
    - <u>Test properties</u> - high Sn for UTI (70-95%)
    - <u>Causes of false +ve</u>:
      - Presence of formalin
      - Contamination by vaginal discharge
    - <u>Causes of false -ve</u>:
      - High specific gravity
      - Dehydration
      - Glycosuria
      - Presence of urobilinogen
      - Test read too late (due to WBC lysis)
  - **Nitrites** - G- bacteria convert nitrates in urine into nitrites:
    - <u>Interpretation</u> - +ve results suggestive of bacteriuria (N.B. not clinical infection if asymptomatic)
    - <u>Test properties</u> - variable Sn (35-85%), high Sp (92-100%)
    - <u>Causes of false +ve</u>:
      - Contamination by vaginal flora
    - <u>Causes of false -ve</u>:
      - UTI by non-fermenters (e.g. Pseudomonas)
      - UTI by GPC (e.g. S. saprophyticus, enterococcus)
- **DDx for sterile pyuria:**
  - Urinary tract stones
  - Genitourinary TB
  - Urothelial carcinoma
  - Schistosomiasis
  - Partially treated UTI
  - Other inflammatory bladder conditions (e.g. interstitial cystitis)
  - Tubulointerstitial nephritis
